ese measures I understand  that some of them are non-public you  must understand we don't want to do this  out of any particular I don't take any  joy in this so we have expelled 23  diplomats probably spies masquerading as  diplomats and there are other things  that we are going to do and in  particular yes we know we're toughening  up our our borders we have plenty of  laws which allow us to crack down on  money in the UK that has been illicit  Leo Alex plant unexplained wealth orders  right that is money that's been illicit  be or corruptly obtained we can do that  we have the National Crime Agency the  National Economic crime unit can can do  that but I must and they are they will  get on with that work but I must stress  to you that the UK is a a country under  the rule of law and I cannot as a  politician say you know go after that  person's cash do this do that that's not  how it works  you're not going to develop any  like the one that was developed by the  United States the so-called clear  criminal East well we are we're sharing  obviously we share intelligence we share  information we do share lists of people  with with friends and partners around  the world but we operate under the can  you say that so it's a matter for for  the criminal law enforcement agency to  determine whether there is a case to  answer can you say right now that all  officials who work for mr. Putin  including Igor Shuvalov he has a  luxurious property in London that he  will be deprived of his property and the  officials like him I think the most the  the worst thing I could do now given the  work that's going on is get into  individual names or cases because all  these individuals have as you know deep  pockets and very powerful lawyers and I  think I'm sure that almost anything I  said about anybody would be taken to be  prejudicial to any case yeah I will I  will try to put it in another way so you  referred to some Russian businessman as  related to Putin so they might be  punished  what criteria will be applied in  determining that one businessman is  related to Putin and another is not well  I again I think you're inviting me very  very rightly but you're inviting me to  go further in into the detail of such  cases and I think it would be wise for  me to do because all all such legal  actions are open to to challenge under  our system and it's very important that  there should be no there should be no  sign of political bias or interference  and and there won't be you said that you  felt a lot of support from the European  Union and the United States do you think  that these countries will take any  radical measures for example they will  free throw out Russia from the United  Nations Security Council or impose new  sanctions we're laughing well I think  because in order to I think in order to  change the composition of the night  Nations Security Council you would need  a unanimous vote of the Security Council  I think Russia is likely to vote in that  way so you know I think we have already  been very struck and surprised actually  and very encouraged by the strength of  support and the strength of feeling and  the level of sympathy for the for the UK  and what we wanted to do is to work with  our friends and partners sharing  intelligence countering disinformation  mounting defenses against cyber warfare  tracking the money trying as we were  just discussing to crack down on people  who may have a listen Leo Krupp they  obtained their wealth there is all sorts  of work that is now going on but the  objective I must stress this point the  objective is not to punish the people of  Russia at all the objective is to try to  show that we care deeply about the kind  of disruptive and dangerous and reckless  behavior reckless with human life that  we've seen from Russia and that we want  to put pressure on the regime to find a  new path and they can find a new path  and there can be cause for hope but  there won't be cause for hope if we  